a.record('pfp/domain/shared_kernel/data.py',1,97,'Read recursive DataRecord freeze/unfreeze/hash. Mapping values are copied to proxies, sequences to tuples, unordered sets and nonfinite floats refused; finite floats are intentionally accepted, so this generic JSON record is not an exact-rational container. Recursive snapshots prevent ordinary nested source-list/dict mutation but have no depth/cycle budget. Equality uses ordinary Python JSON-like value equality, which identifies bool/int/float numeric equivalents and list/tuple inputs. Runtime hash is not a typed canonical digest. Consumers must choose their own wire/type fidelity policy.',['recursive snapshot','finite float allowance','numeric equality policy','not canonical digest'])
a.record('pfp/domain/shared_kernel/byte_payload.py',1,56,'Read16MiB byte payload limit, string-length guard, immutable copy and computed ContentDigest. Bytearray is copied so later mutation does not change the sealed payload. Limit is on already supplied encoded payload, not decompressed/transformed output or preallocation; string length counts code points then final bytes are checked. Module-local64/256 candidate/operation constants differ from generic budget limits1024/65536 and are not themselves enforced here. UTF8 decoding/encoding failures remain ordinary exceptions.',['byte custody','postallocation bound','representation-size limit','context-specific budget constants'])
a.record('pfp/domain/shared_kernel/bytes.py',1,72,'Read nonnegative ByteCount, exact-byte/string factories, comparisons and nominal equality/hash. Count constructors reject bool but accept int subclasses, factories require exact int. String count uses actual encoded byte length. is_within_limit trusts the supplied limit type/range and only compares; it does not meter I/O or enforce allocation.',['byte measure','factory strictness','declared ceiling'])
a.record('pfp/domain/shared_kernel/hash.py',1,88,'Read generic hash and SHA256 nominal classes. Constructor intentionally strips surrounding whitespace and lowercases before regex validation, unlike the stricter identifiers.Sha256Digest representation. SHA256 computations bind raw supplied bytes/encoded string, with nominal class equality and runtime hash. Generic Hash permits any lowercase hexadecimal length32-128, not a fixed registered algorithm. No proof/provenance semantics follow from successful format validation.',['digest normalization','nominal identity','algorithm label ceiling'])
a.record('pfp/domain/shared_kernel/content_digest.py',1,70,'Read normalized ContentDigest constructor and exact bytes/string digest factories. Whitespace/case normalization is a convenience boundary; digest generation and comparison otherwise use SHA256 and exact nominal type. Existing provided digest values are not linked to content by constructor alone; that binding is supplied by BytePayload/from_bytes callers. Factory strictness differs from direct isinstance-str constructor.',['content binding caller','normalized digest representation','exact factory inputs'])
a.record('pfp/domain/shared_kernel/path.py',1,149,'Read syntactic POSIX/Windows-sensitive path checks and nominal owned/protected paths. Strict validation refuses traversal, absolute/double/trailing slashes, drives/streams, controls, trailing dots/spaces and common reserved devices. is_normalized_relative_posix is weaker: only NUL is a control check, unlike validate_path_security. is_within uses segment-aware prefix but trusts/coerces its root; startswith remains lexical. No symlink/casefold/filesystem resolution or actual permission enforcement occurs; OwnedPath/ProtectedPath are labels, so I/O adapters must enforce resolved workspace bounds.',['syntactic path checks','predicate mismatch','containment boundary','ownership labels not permissions'])
a.record('pfp/domain/shared_kernel/yaml_strict.py',1,124,'Read SafeLoader subclass, mapping/boolean constructors and entry points. Mapping flattening rejects duplicate/equality-colliding keys after construction and unsafe YAML object tags remain excluded by SafeLoader. Only constructors are changed; inherited scalar resolvers remain, so a full YAML1.2 schema claim is stronger than this implementation. Boolean-tagged yes/no/on/off variants are refused, but tokens not resolved to bool bypass that branch; y/n list entries alone do not ensure rejection. Arbitrary hashable mapping keys, aliases/recursive/deep data and nonfinite numeric scalar tags are not excluded here. Downstream schemas must enforce their own types/size and exactness.',['safe loader ceiling','duplicate keys','resolver inheritance','downstream scalar checks'])
